\section{Introduction}

The students present their labs (\enquote{redovisning}) online, to the TAs who
have booked the presentation sessions in nytid.
In Canvas the students book a time for this in an appointment group: the
session is cut into slots, 15 minutes for a lab and 30 for the P-uppgift, and
each slot takes a fixed number of students.
For the session on 5 October, 13:00--15:00, we want
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms calendar create -c prgi26 "Redovisning" \
    --start-time "2026-10-05 13:00" --end-time "2026-10-05 15:00" \
    --location "https://queue.eecs.kth.se/queue/Prgi" \
    --event-type appointment_group \
    --time-slot-duration 15 --max-appointments 4 \
    --description "marwahas samed mweng yasmines ealaton kfgruner"
\end{minted}
Six TAs have booked that session (besides me), so four students per slot.

This script derives such commands from the TA schedule in nytid.
It only \emph{prints} them: I review, edit and run them myself.
Creating an appointment group is not idempotent---running the same command
twice gives the students two groups to book in---so the script must never be
the one that sends them to Canvas.

We publish the slots of a session at least a week before it, but not much
earlier: further ahead, TAs still book and unbook sessions, so the number of
students per slot would be based on a staffing that has not yet stabilized.

\subsection{Running it weekly from cron}

That makes the script a good fit for a weekly cron job, for instance on
Monday mornings:
\begin{minted}{text}
0 8 * * 1 cd ~/devel/edu/intropy/adm/redovisningar && ./redovisningar.sh prgi26
\end{minted}
Cron mails me the output.
It starts with what Canvas already holds for those days, see
\cref{sec:canvas}, followed by the commands.
I open the mail in Vim, check each command against the overview, select it
and run it with
[[:'<,'>w !bash]], which writes the selected lines to [[bash]] and leaves
the mail as it is.
The script still only prints; running a command remains my decision.

Each run covers the seven days that start a week after the day it runs, see
\cref{sec:schedule}: the run on Monday 12 October covers 19--25 October.
So every session is published 7 to 13 days before it takes place, and
consecutive weekly runs cover consecutive weeks, with neither overlap nor
gap.
No session is printed twice, so running the commands of every mail never
gives a session two appointment groups, and none is missed.
The defaults are chosen for this use: the window is the week that starts a week from
today, and
each session's title, and with it the slot length, follows from its event in
nytid.
A week of P-uppgift presentations thus gets 30-minute slots without a change
to the cron line, which needs no more than the course.

\subsection{The script}

The structure follows [[missing.sh]]: constants, functions and a [[main]]
that runs only when the script is executed, not when it is sourced for
debugging.
The script is run as
\begin{minted}{text}
redovisningar.sh [-d minutes] course [title] [nytid schedule show options]
\end{minted}
The course is used for both nytid and canvaslms.
The title is the name of the appointment group in Canvas.
By default each session gets its title from its event in nytid; a title given
here replaces it for every session of the run.
The title decides the slot length, which [[-d]] overrides, see
\cref{sec:title}.
The remaining arguments go to [[nytid schedule show]], see
\cref{sec:schedule}.
<<[[redovisningar.sh]]>>=
#!/bin/bash

<<constants>>
<<functions>>

# Run the script if not sourced
if [[ "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" == "${0}" ]]; then
  main "$@"
fi
@

A typical run by hand is
\begin{minted}{bash}
./redovisningar.sh prgi26 > redovisningar-v41.sh
\end{minted}
followed by editing the output and running it with [[bash]].
To tell the students which lab they present, and to give the slots another
length than the title gives, say 20 minutes, we run
\begin{minted}{bash}
./redovisningar.sh -d 20 prgi26 "Redovisning lab 3"
\end{minted}


\section{The presentation sessions}\label{sec:schedule}

[[nytid schedule show]] shows the events from today until a week from today
by default.
That is not the window we want: we publish a week ahead, so the window starts
[[LEAD_DAYS]] days from today and lasts [[WINDOW_DAYS]] days.
nytid includes the events of both dates, so the end date is
[[WINDOW_DAYS - 1]] days after the start.
Had we used the start plus a week as the end, as nytid's own default does,
the window would be eight days, and the weekly cron job would print the
sessions of that eighth day twice: as the last day of one run and the first
of the next.

We put our [[--start]] and [[--end]] before the user's arguments: nytid, like
any Click-based program, takes the last value of an option given twice, so
the same options among the extra arguments override ours.
Extra arguments, such as [[--start 2026-10-12 --end 2026-10-18]], are passed
on to nytid that way.
When moving the window, give both dates: with only [[--start]], our end date
could fall before the start, and nytid then replaces it with the start plus a
week, which is eight days again.

We ask for [[--user ""]] to get all sessions and every TA booked on them, not
only my own bookings.
We ask for CSV with the end time and full dates, since canvaslms wants
[[YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM]] for both ends of the session.
We also state [[--no-week]], [[--no-location]] and the delimiter explicitly,
although some of them are defaults: each of them can be changed in the nytid
configuration, and a changed default would shift the columns that we read
below.
<<constants>>=
TIME_FORMAT="%Y-%m-%d %H:%M"
LEAD_DAYS=7
WINDOW_DAYS=7
<<functions>>=
sessions() {
  nytid schedule show "$course" --user "" \
    --format csv --delimiter $'\t' \
    --no-week --no-location --no-todo \
    --end-time --time-format "$TIME_FORMAT" \
    --start "$(date -d "+$LEAD_DAYS days" +%F)" \
    --end "$(date -d "+$((LEAD_DAYS + WINDOW_DAYS - 1)) days" +%F)" \
    "$@" \
  | tr -d '\r' \
  | awk -F'\t' -v pattern="$SESSION_PATTERN" \
    'tolower($3) ~ pattern'
}
@

Python's [[csv]] module ends every row with CRLF, which [[tr]] removes;
otherwise the last field, and with it the last TA's username, would end in
a carriage return.

The rows then have the columns start, end, event and description.
The calendar holds more than the TA sign-up sheet: meetings and lectures,
some of them about the presentations, such as \enquote{Möte inför
redovisningarna}.
Matching \enquote{redovisning} anywhere in the event name would turn such a
meeting into an appointment group.
The sessions of the sign-up sheet are instead the events whose name starts
with \enquote{Datorlaboration}, so we keep only those; which of them are
presentations, and of what, the rest of the name tells, see
\cref{sec:title}.
We match on the event column only, since the description could mention a
lab without being one.
<<constants>>=
SESSION_PATTERN="^datorlaboration"
@


\section{The TAs of a session}

The description of a session looks like
\begin{minted}{text}
Needed TAs: 10; Booked TAs: dbosk marwahas samed mweng yasmines ealaton kfgruner
\end{minted}
and may continue with [[; Reserve TAs: ...]].
Reserves are not counted: they only work the session if a booked TA drops out.
We take the words between [[Booked TAs:]] and the next semicolon.

I am booked on the sessions too, but I do not take presentations, so I am
excluded both from the count and from the list of TAs in the description.
nytid identifies TAs by their KTH username, which on my machine is also
[[$USER]]; [[TEACHER]] can be set in the environment to override it.

A TA can appear twice in the list when they have booked the same session twice
in the sign-up sheet, as happened on 6 October 2026.
They still take only one student at a time, so we keep only the first
occurrence of each name ([[awk '!seen[$0]++']] keeps the order, unlike
[[sort -u]]).
<<constants>>=
TEACHER="${TEACHER:-$USER}"
<<functions>>=
booked_tas() {
  local description="$1"
  local ta
  [[ $description =~ Booked\ TAs:\ ([^;]*) ]] || return
  for ta in ${BASH_REMATCH[1]}; do
    if [ "$ta" != "$TEACHER" ]; then
      echo "$ta"
    fi
  done \
  | awk '!seen[$0]++'
}
@


\section{Students per slot}

Each slot should take fewer students than there are TAs: we save more than
20~per cent of the TAs' capacity.
That is, the number of students per slot is the largest integer strictly less
than \(0.8n\) for \(n\) TAs, which is \(\lfloor (4n - 1)/5 \rfloor\) in integer
arithmetic.
Six TAs give four students per slot, ten TAs give seven.
The plain \(\lfloor 4n/5 \rfloor\) would give four students for five TAs,
which saves exactly 20~per cent, not more.
<<functions>>=
students_per_slot() {
  local tas="$1"
  echo $(( (4 * tas - 1) / 5 ))
}
@

With no TA other than me, this is zero (or negative).
That happens, for instance, when only reserves have signed up besides me.
Canvas has no use for a slot that takes no students, so [[main]] prints a
comment instead of a command for such a session; being a comment, it does no
harm when the output is run.


\section{The title and the slot length}\label{sec:title}

We book several kinds of presentations: the labs and the stages of the
P-uppgift.
The title of the appointment group tells the students which one they book,
for example \enquote{Redovisning} or \enquote{Redovisning P-uppgift}.

The event names in nytid already say which kind a session is.
After \enquote{Datorlaboration} comes the kind, and possibly
\enquote{online}:
\enquote{Datorlaboration redovisning online} is a lab presentation, and
\enquote{Datorlaboration redovisning p-uppgift} would be a P-uppgift one.
So we take the title from what follows \enquote{Datorlaboration}.
We drop \enquote{online}: it says where the session is, which the location
already tells the students, not what they present.
A kind that does not start with \enquote{redovisning}, such as the empty one
of \enquote{Datorlaboration}, \enquote{online} or \enquote{Excel}, is an
ordinary lab session, not a presentation, and gives no title.
We capitalize the first letter, since the kind starts a title in Canvas.
<<functions>>=
session_title() {
  local event="$1" kind
  kind=$(sed -E 's/^datorlaboration *//I; s/ *online$//I' <<< "$event")
  if [[ ${kind,,} == redovisning* ]]; then
    echo "${kind^}"
  fi
}
@

A filter, [[presentations]], drops the sessions without a title and passes
the other rows through unchanged.
They must go before [[main]] looks at the rows at all: a week of only
ordinary lab sessions should give no output, so that cron sends no mail,
see \cref{sec:canvas}.
<<functions>>=
presentations() {
  local start end event description
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r start end event description; do
    if [ -n "$(session_title "$event")" ]; then
      printf '%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$start" "$end" "$event" "$description"
    fi
  done
}
@

A title given on the command line replaces the derived one for all
presentation sessions of the run, for instance to name the lab, as in
\enquote{Redovisning lab 3}.
It does not make an ordinary lab session a presentation: the event still
decides which sessions get an appointment group.

The kind of presentation also decides how long a slot should be.
A lab and the shorter stages of the P-uppgift, the specification
(\enquote{spec}) and the review (\enquote{granskning}), take 15 minutes.
Presenting the P-uppgift itself, a whole program, takes 30.
We derive the slot length from the title, so that the event in nytid, or
the title we give, is all it takes in the usual cases:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lr}
  Title & Minutes \\
  \hline
  Redovisning & 15 \\
  Redovisning lab 3 & 15 \\
  Redovisning spec & 15 \\
  Redovisning granskning & 15 \\
  Redovisning P-uppgift & 30 \\
  Redovisning p-uppgift del 2 & 30 \\
  anything else & 15 \\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The 15-minute rule gives the same as the default, but stating it records
which titles we know about.
A title outside both rules gets the default rather than an error, since the
commands are reviewed before they are run.

The regular expressions need care with anchoring.
The one for the 15-minute titles is anchored at the end: otherwise its
optional group would let it match every title that starts with
\enquote{Redovisning}, the P-uppgift ones included.
The one for the P-uppgift is anchored only at the start, so that a title
with more after it, such as a part number, still gets 30 minutes.
<<constants>>=
LAB_TITLES='^Redovisning ?(lab.*|spec|granskning)?$'
LAB_SLOT_DURATION=15
PROJECT_TITLES='^Redovisning [Pp]-uppgift'
PROJECT_SLOT_DURATION=30
DEFAULT_SLOT_DURATION=15
@

The expressions are kept in variables and used unquoted after [[=~]]: a
quoted right-hand side would be matched as a literal string, and writing the
expression inline would force us to escape its spaces.
<<functions>>=
slot_duration_for() {
  local title="$1"
  if [[ $title =~ $PROJECT_TITLES ]]; then
    echo "$PROJECT_SLOT_DURATION"
  elif [[ $title =~ $LAB_TITLES ]]; then
    echo "$LAB_SLOT_DURATION"
  else
    echo "$DEFAULT_SLOT_DURATION"
  fi
}
@


\section{The canvaslms command}

The location is the same for every session.
It is the queue system, where the students queue to the TA once their slot
starts.
<<constants>>=
LOCATION="https://queue.eecs.kth.se/queue/Prgi"
@

We print the command in the same layout as in the introduction, one option
group per line, so that it is easy to edit before running it.
The TAs go in the description; that way the students see who is working the
session.
The course is the same for all sessions of a run, so [[print_command]]
reads it as [[main]] set it; the title and the slot length can differ between
sessions and are passed along with the rest.
<<functions>>=
print_command() {
  local start="$1" end="$2" title="$3" duration="$4" max="$5" tas="$6"
  printf 'canvaslms calendar create -c %s "%s" \\\n' "$course" "$title"
  printf '    --start-time "%s" --end-time "%s" \\\n' "$start" "$end"
  printf '    --location "%s" \\\n' "$LOCATION"
  printf '    --event-type appointment_group \\\n'
  printf '    --time-slot-duration %s --max-appointments %s \\\n' \
    "$duration" "$max"
  printf '    --description "%s"\n' "$tas"
}
@


\section{What is already in Canvas}\label{sec:canvas}

Before I run a command, I want to see whether the session already has an
appointment group, made by hand or by an earlier run with another window.
Running the command anyway would give the students two groups to book in.
So the output starts with what Canvas already holds over the days of the
sessions, followed by the commands.
The overview lines are comments, like the skipped sessions, so they do no
harm if the whole output is run.

[[canvaslms calendar list]] prints one row per slot, with the columns title,
start, end and booked/capacity:
\begin{minted}{text}
Redovisning  2026-10-05 13:15  2026-10-05 13:30  4/4
Redovisning  2026-10-05 13:30  2026-10-05 13:45  4/4
\end{minted}
A two-hour session is eight such rows, so we fold the slots of one
appointment group into one line:
\begin{minted}{text}
#   Redovisning  2026-10-05 13:15-15:00  7 slots  28/28 booked
\end{minted}
Canvas does not tell us which group a slot belongs to, so we take a run of
slots with the same title and capacity, each starting where the previous one
ended, to be one group.
Two groups of the same title and capacity that follow each other without a
pause are thus shown as one line; that does not hide whether the time is
covered, which is what the overview is for.
Two groups over the same time, on the other hand, interleave their slots and
break the runs, so they show up as a stretch of one-slot lines, which is hard
to miss.
We ask for [[--type event]] so that assignment deadlines stay out.
<<functions>>=
canvas_overview() {
  local from="$1" to="$2"
  echo "# Already in Canvas, $from to $to:"
  canvaslms calendar list -c "$course" --type event \
    --start-time "$from" --end-time "$to 23:59" \
  | awk -F'\t' '
      function flush() {
        if (n)
          printf "#   %s  %s-%s  %d slots  %d/%d booked\n",
            title, first, substr(last, 12), n, booked, capacity
      }
      {
        split($4, count, "/")
        if (n && $1 == title && $2 == last && count[2] == size) {
          last = $3; n++
        } else {
          flush()
          title = $1; first = $2; last = $3; size = count[2]; n = 1
          booked = capacity = 0
        }
        booked += count[1]; capacity += count[2]
      }
      END { if (n) flush(); else print "#   nothing" }'
}
@


\section{The main function}

[[main]] reads the arguments, fetches the sessions, shows what Canvas already
holds over their days and prints one command per presentation session, with
a blank line between them.
nytid returns the sessions in time order, so the first row's start and the
last row's end give the days for the Canvas overview.
With no presentation sessions, nothing is printed at all, and cron sends no
mail.
For each session, [[main]] first settles the title and the slot length, from
the event unless the command line overrides them, see \cref{sec:title}.
<<functions>>=
main() {
  <<read the arguments>>
  local rows start end event description kind title duration tas n max
  rows=$(sessions "$@" | presentations)
  [ -n "$rows" ] || return 0
  canvas_overview "$(head -n 1 <<< "$rows" | cut -f1 | cut -d' ' -f1)" \
    "$(tail -n 1 <<< "$rows" | cut -f2 | cut -d' ' -f1)"
  echo
  echo "# To create:"
  echo
  echo "$rows" \
  | while IFS=$'\t' read -r start end event description; do
      kind=$(session_title "$event")
      title="${title_override:-$kind}"
      duration="${duration_override:-$(slot_duration_for "$title")}"
      tas=$(booked_tas "$description" | tr '\n' ' ')
      tas="${tas% }"
      n=$(wc -w <<< "$tas")
      max=$(students_per_slot "$n")
      if [ "$max" -lt 1 ]; then
        echo "# $start to $end $event: no TA but $TEACHER, skipping"
      else
        print_command "$start" "$end" "$title" "$duration" "$max" "$tas"
      fi
      echo
    done
}
@

\subsection{Reading the arguments}\label{sec:arguments}

[[-d]] must come before the course.
Everything after the title goes to nytid unchanged, so an option there is
nytid's; [[getopts]] stops at the first argument that is not an option
anyway.
[[OPTIND]] is local so that [[main]] can be called more than once when the
script is sourced for debugging.
We accept only a positive whole number of minutes, since a slot of zero
minutes, or of a fraction of one, means nothing to canvaslms.
<<read the arguments>>=
local opt OPTIND duration_override=""
while getopts d: opt; do
  case "$opt" in
    d) duration_override="$OPTARG" ;;
    *) usage; return 1 ;;
  esac
done
shift $((OPTIND - 1))
if [[ -n $duration_override && ! $duration_override =~ ^[1-9][0-9]*$ ]]
then
  usage
  return 1
fi
@

The course is required.
The title is optional, yet it comes before the options to nytid.
We tell the two apart by the leading dash: everything we give nytid is an
option, such as [[--start]], and no title starts with a dash.
That keeps the cron line short, and a run by hand can still move the window
without naming the title.
<<read the arguments>>=
if [ $# -lt 1 ]; then
  usage
  return 1
fi
course="$1"
shift
local title_override=""
if [[ -n $1 && $1 != -* ]]; then
  title_override="$1"
  shift
fi
@

The usage message goes to standard error, so that it never ends up among the
commands when the output is redirected to a file or mailed by cron.
[[main]] returns after it rather than exits, which keeps a shell that sourced
the script alive.
<<functions>>=
usage() {
  echo "usage: ${0##*/} [-d minutes] course [title]" \
    "[nytid schedule show options]" >&2
}
@

